---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import logoMark from '../assets/logo.svg?raw';
---
<MainLayout
  title="About"
  description="Personal background, language learning turning points, structural overview of the archive, and the typographic design principles of this publication."
>
  <PageHead
    kicker="About / 03"
    title="About"
    deck="The author, the method, and the modes of this publication."
    asideLines={['The author', 'Est. 2024', 'Vol. I / 2026']}
    glyph="about"
  />

  <section class="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
    <div class="wrap about-grid">
      <div class="about-main">
        <h2 class="about-title" id="about-title">A record of what I am trying to understand</h2>

        <p class="about-copy">
          I am Duzui, also known as duzui9039 or duzuictx. I read, write, and think across three
          languages—<span lang="en">English</span>, <span lang="zh-Hans">普通话</span>, and{' '}
          <span lang="ja">日本語</span>—and am currently exploring <span lang="es">español</span>.
          I began my university studies in theoretical physics, which trained me to value mathematical
          rigor and first principles. Looking to connect abstract models with tangible systems, I spent
          several years independently studying computer science and artificial intelligence, eventually
          securing admission to graduate school in computer science. Outside of computation, my long-term
          interests center on linguistics, classical texts, and building quiet software tools for reading
          and study.
        </p>

        <p class="about-copy">
          My experience with language taught me to distrust purely mechanical practice. When standard drills
          and grammar rulebooks failed to turn into genuine communication, I stepped away from test prep and
          began engaging with living material: reading original non-fiction, digging through shelves in
          secondhand bookstores, and having unscripted conversations with native speakers in VRChat. Later,
          living in Japan for half a year as an exchange student reinforced that language only becomes real
          when it is lived daily rather than studied for an exam. More importantly, speaking directly with
          concrete individuals quietly dissolved the abstract labels and distant assumptions I had once held.
        </p>

        <p class="about-copy">
          This site is organized into four separate sections: Notes, Thoughts, Research, and Projects.
          Notes hold reading fragments, citations, and working records. Thoughts gather personal reflections
          and cultural essays that are allowed to remain unfinished and imperfect. Research contains formal
          notes and technical writing in physics and computation. Projects document the small tools I build
          for myself—such as reading workstations for foreign and classical languages—crafted around personal
          study habits rather than commercial metrics. Keeping them distinct keeps the record honest: study
          notes do not pretend to be polished essays, and private tools do not pretend to be commercial
          products.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="about-side" id="colophon">
        <figure class="logo-plate" role="img" aria-label="Duzui, Cosmo in Me" set:html={logoMark}></figure>
        <p class="colophon">
          <strong>Colophon</strong><br />
          Set in Cormorant Garamond, EB Garamond, Source Serif 4, IBM Plex Sans, and IBM Plex Mono. Built
          with Astro. The design sources are classical humanism, mathematical geometry, astronomical
          romanticism, and modern science fiction.
        </p>
        <div class="elsewhere">
          <p class="smallcaps">Elsewhere</p>
          <ul>
            {
              site.social.map((item) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={item.href} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                    {item.label}
                    <span>↗</span>
                  </a>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="modes-legend">
          <p class="smallcaps">A note on the modes</p>
          <div class="mode-row">
            <svg class="mode-glyph" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="8" cy="8" r="5.5" /></svg>
            <div><span class="mode-name">The Library</span><span class="mode-desc">Warm paper, serif, editorial space. For notes, thoughts, and reading.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="mode-row">
            <svg class="mode-glyph" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="8" cy="8" rx="6.5" ry="4" /></svg>
            <div><span class="mode-name">The Observatory</span><span class="mode-desc">Deep blue, thin geometry, coordinates. For research and formal ideas.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="mode-row">
            <svg class="mode-glyph" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 2 L14 13 L2 13 Z" /></svg>
            <div><span class="mode-name">The Frontier</span><span class="mode-desc">Ink, abstraction, asymmetry. For projects and experiments.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="about-plate">
          <img
            src="/images/about-vrchat.webp"
            alt="A space station orbiting above Earth"
            width="1200"
            height="675"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </figure>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="timeline-head">
        <p class="smallcaps">Timeline / A short history of the archive</p>
        <h2 id="timeline-title">Six dates, one record</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="timeline-list">
        <li class="timeline-item"><div class="year">2024</div><div class="event">First personal blog</div><div class="event-note">Started an initial blog to document reading notes, language learning, and technical workflows.</div></li>
        <li class="timeline-item"><div class="year">2024</div><div class="event">Exchange in Japan</div><div class="event-note">Spent half a year living in Japan, experiencing academic research environments and cross-cultural communication.</div></li>
        <li class="timeline-item"><div class="year">2025</div><div class="event">Physics senior thesis</div><div class="event-note">Formulated an extension of the Euclidean path integral for thermofield double states in curved spacetime.</div></li>
        <li class="timeline-item"><div class="year">2026</div><div class="event">Graduate studies admission</div><div class="event-note">Self-studied computer science and artificial intelligence, securing admission to graduate studies in Japan.</div></li>
        <li class="timeline-item"><div class="year">2026</div><div class="event">Started building things</div><div class="event-note">Began Diglota and Tablinum, two language tools made for my own reading and study.</div></li>
        <li class="timeline-item"><div class="year">2026</div><div class="event">Catalogue of Curiosities</div><div class="event-note">Launched this publication in Astro with bespoke typographic modes and constellation maps.</div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</MainLayout>
